Tell me how the story 
is always beginning without a voice  
and your hands try rearranging the dark
and your body is the radio sitting on the shelf, 
taken apart to find the voice
inside its head. 

Back to the bridge where our knees 
stick to the concrete pleading for flight
but our spines always respect gravity.
Every star was some headstone you dug 
out of that night. How the sound
of your friend asking you to breathe in
missed your ear like a bullet.
 
It is not a story you have to believe 
It is a dream you don't remember.

Look at the light washing the lungs 
of the dark, as if saying time is not a morgue.
 
In the sea, you thought you were drowning 
but the water christened you instead.

 




